From: Dmytro-K <dimattt92@googlemail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] iio: buffer: add iio_buffer_get_from_file() and block claiming

Let other kernel code (the iqnet zerocopy UDP streamer) take over the
legacy DMA blocks of an IIO buffer that userspace allocated through an
open /dev/iio:deviceN file descriptor.

iio_buffer_get_from_file() returns the primary buffer behind such a file,
with a reference taken. Only files opened through iio_buffer_fileops are
accepted: f_op is compared before private_data is trusted, so an arbitrary
user supplied fd is safe. Anonymous IIO_BUFFER_GET_FD_IOCTL buffer fds,
event-only device files (iio_event_fileops) and anything else get
-EINVAL. iio_buffer_fileops is static in industrialio-core.c, hence the
small IIO-core-internal helper iio_chrdev_file_to_buffer() (iio_core.h).

iio_buffer_claim_blocks() / iio_buffer_release_blocks() hand the blocks
to the in-kernel user and back. While claimed, -EBUSY is returned by:
- IIO_BLOCK_ALLOC/FREE/QUERY/ENQUEUE/DEQUEUE_IOCTL on that buffer
  (through the chardev or an anon fd) and IIO_BUFFER_GET_FD_IOCTL for it;
- writing 0 to its sysfs buffer/enable, whether or not it is enabled
  (writing 1 is still allowed: the owner enables the buffer after
  claiming it, so the first DMA block already belongs to it);
- writing its buffer/length or buffer/watermark (even the current value)
  and its scan_elements/*_en attributes.
Claiming fails with -EBUSY if already claimed or if an anon buffer fd is
open for it.

The sysfs part matters because a second userspace user would otherwise
tear the stream down: libiio's local_open(), which iiod runs for every
network client creating an RX buffer, writes buffer/enable=0,
buffer/length and buffer/watermark before it even opens the chardev. The
disable aborted the owner's in-flight DMA and the stream stalled for good;
now that client fails up front with -EBUSY. In-kernel
iio_update_buffers() callers and device unregistration are not blocked.

Locking: the claim is a new bit in iio_buffer::flags (next to
IIO_BUSY_BIT_POS), changed and tested under a new per-buffer mutex,
iio_buffer::claim_lock. info_exist_lock, which serialises the ioctls,
cannot be used: it lives in iio_dev_opaque and the claim API only gets
the buffer, and a bare test_and_set_bit() would not wait for a block
ioctl that already passed its test. iio_buffer_ioctl() therefore holds
claim_lock (nested inside info_exist_lock) across the whole block
operation, and the sysfs stores above hold it (nested inside mlock)
across theirs, so once iio_buffer_claim_blocks() returns no such
userspace operation is in progress. A blocking DEQUEUE drops claim_lock
together with info_exist_lock while it sleeps (so claiming never waits
for data) and re-checks the claim after retaking both. The anon fd busy
bit is set in iio_device_buffer_getfd() under claim_lock as well, so the
two cannot both succeed.

Lock order is info_exist_lock -> mlock -> claim_lock (matching the
existing info_exist_lock -> mlock of iio_update_buffers()). claim_lock is
innermost: nothing takes mlock or info_exist_lock with it held; the block
operations run under it only take the buffer implementation's own locks.

This also fixes the existing DEQUEUE sleep path returning on a signal
with info_exist_lock dropped, which made its callers unlock a mutex they
no longer held: both locks are now retaken before every return.

The buffer reference only keeps struct iio_buffer alive. The legacy block
API frees all blocks when the chardev file is released, and the file holds
the IIO device reference, so a user of the blocks must also keep a
reference to the file for as long as it uses them.
